    body: ["General Introduction\nThe Vijayanagar Kingdom\nSangama Dynasty Tuluva Dynasty Saluva Dynasty Aravidu Dynasty\nHarihara and Bukka \n(c.1336-1377 CE) Vir Narsingh Narasimha Deva Raya Tirumala Deva Raya\nFig 5.1: Vijayanagar Kingdom\nThe Vijayanagar kingdom, spanning from approximately \n1336 to the end of the 17th century, was characterized by the \nrule of four prominent dynasties: Sangama (1336–1485 CE), \nSaluva (1485–1503 CE), Tuluva (1503–1570 CE), and Aravidu \n(until the late 17th century). A comprehensive understanding \nof this kingdom is derived from diverse sources, including \nliterary and archaeological findings, as well as numismatics.\nFig 5.2: Map of Deccan Sultanate and Vijayanagar Empire\n Literary Sources: Indigenous literature from the period, \nsuch as Krishnadevaraya’s Amuktamalyada, Gangadevi’s \nMaduravijayam, and Allasani Peddanna’s Manucharitam, \ncontribute significantly to our knowledge.\n• Additionally, accounts of foreign travelers like Ibn \nBattuta, Nicolo de Conti, Abdur Razzak, and Domingo \nPaes offer valuable insights into the socioeconomic \nconditions of the Vijayanagar kingdom.\n Inscriptions and other Architectural Sources: Copper￾plate inscriptions, exemplified by the Srirangam \ncopper plates of Devaraya II, play a crucial role in \nproviding genealogical information and detailing the \naccomplishments of Vijayanagar rulers.\nMap of Deccan Sultanate and Vijayanagar Empire\n The architectural remains at Hampi and other \nmonuments associated with Vijayanagar offer insights \ninto the cultural contributions of its rulers.\n The extensive coinage issued by Vijayanagar rulers \nfeatures figures and inscriptions explaining their titles \nand achievements.\n It’s noteworthy that the Vijayanagar empire was a diverse \namalgamation of people from various cultural regions, \nincluding Tamil, Telugu, and Karnataka, who spoke different \nlanguages and embraced distinct cultural practices.\n Expansion and Challenges in the South: In the southern \nregion, the Vijayanagara Empire faced its primary \nadversaries in the form of the Sultans of Madurai, and this \nconflict persisted for nearly four decades. By around 1377 \nCE, the Vijayanagara Kingdom had emerged victorious, \nleading to the complete elimination of the Sultanate \nof Madurai. Consequently, Vijayanagara expanded its \ndominion to encompass the entire southern part of \nIndia, stretching up to Rameshwaram. This included \nnot only the Tamil country but also the territories of the \nCheras in Kerala.\nVijayanagar Empire and \nBahamani Kingdoms 5"]
