    body: ["74 Medieval India \n Rivalries in the North: In the Northern front, the \nVijayanagara Empire engaged in sustained hostilities \nwith the Bahmani Sultanate. This Northern rivalry was \nmarked by continuous conflict between the two powers \nas they vied for dominance in the region. This geopolitical \ndynamic on both the southern and Northern fronts \ncontributed to the complex history of the Vijayanagara \nEmpire, shaping its territorial boundaries and influencing \nits interactions with neighbouring states.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nThe territorial expansion and diverse cultural \namalgamation of the Vijayanagara Empire under \nDeva Raya II contributed to a harmonious and \nintegrated socio-political environment. In your \nopinion, did it also lead to challenges in governance \nand administration? Substantiate.\nSANGAMA DYNASTY\nHarihara and Bukka (c. 1336–1377 CE)\n According to legends, the founders of the Sangama \ndynasty of the Vijayanagara Kingdom were two brothers, \nHarihara I and Bukka Raya I. Belonging to a family of \nfive brothers, they initially served as feudatories of the \nKakatiyas of Warangal. Following the fall of the Kakatiya \ndynasty, the brothers became ministers in the Kampili\nstate, located in modern Karnataka.\n When Kampili was captured by Muhammad Bin Tughlaq\nfor harbouring a Muslim rebel, Harihara I and Bukka Raya \nI was imprisoned and subsequently converted to Islam. \nHowever, they later returned to the Hindu faith under \nthe influence of the saint Vidyaranya. \n In honor of their guru and as an assertion of their \nindependence, the brothers founded a new city named \nVidyanagar or Vijayanagara, meaning the ‘city of \nvictory,’ with their capital at Hampi on the southern bank \nof the Tungabhadra River. This marked the establishment \nof the Vijayanagara Empire under the Sangama dynasty \nin 1336.\n The waning power of the Hoysala kingdom provided an \nopportunity for Harihara and Bukka to extend their newly \nestablished Vijayanagara kingdom. By approximately \n1346 CE, they had successfully incorporated the entire \nHoysala territory into their dominion. It’s worth noting \nthat their brothers played a significant role during this \nexpansion, actively engaging in the administration of the \nconquered regions. Consequently, the early Vijayanagara \nkingdom took on the characteristics of a cooperative \ncommonwealth.\n Around 1356 CE, Bukka assumed leadership following \nthe death of his brother Harihara, ruling until 1377 CE. \nDuring his reign, the Vijayanagara forces, with the support \nof their brothers and relatives, effectively eliminated \nthe Sultanate of Madurai. This marked a significant \nachievement in the consolidation and expansion of the \nVijayanagara Empire during Bukka’s rule.\nFig 5.3: Expansion Under Harihara\nHarihara II (c.1377–1404 CE)\n Expansion towards the Eastern Coast: During Harihara \nII’s reign, the Vijayanagara Empire strategically expanded \ntowards the Eastern coast, engaging in conflicts with the \nReddis of Kondavidu to gain control over the Andhra \nregion between Nellore and Kalinga. \n• The empire successfully captured Addanki, Srisailam, \nand a significant portion south of the Krishna River,\nleading to confrontations with the Velamas of \nRachakonda in Telangana. \n• This expansion defined the geographical reach and \ninfluence of the Vijayanagara Empire.\n Resilience against Bahmani–Warangal Alliance: Despite \nchallenges from the Bahmani–Warangal alliance,\nHarihara II displayed resilience in maintaining his position. \nHe achieved success by conquering Belgaum and Goa in \nthe Western region from the Bahmani Sultanate, marking \na crucial chapter in the Vijayanagara Empire’s expansion \nand influence.\n Bahmani Sultanate’s Role and Warangal’s Allegiance: \nThe founder of the Bahmani Sultanate was previously \nsupported by the ruler of Warangal against Delhi. \nHowever, in a twist of events, the successor to the ruler of \nWarangal invaded the city, seizing strategic strongholds, \nincluding the Kaulas and the hill fort. The enduring \nalliance between Warangal and the Bahmani Sultanate \nplayed a significant role in shaping the Vijayanagara \nEmpire’s dominance in the Tungabhadra Doab region."]
