    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms 75\n Geopolitical Dynamics and Empire’s Expansion: \nHarihara II’s accomplishment in wresting control of \ncrucial territories in the west from the Bahmani Sultanate \nmarked a pivotal chapter in the geopolitical dynamics \nof the time. This success substantially contributed to \nthe overall expansion and influence of the Vijayanagara \nEmpire, solidifying its position in the region.\nDeva Raya I (c.1406–1422 CE)\n Military Conflicts and Contributions: \n• Deva Raya’s leadership was marked by continuous \nmilitary engagement. He found himself in frequent \nconflicts with various adversaries, including the \nVelamas of Telangana, the Bahmani Sultan of \nGulbarga, the Reddis of Kondavidu, and the \nGajapatis of Kalinga—traditional rivals of the \nVijayanagara Empire. Despite the challenges posed \nby these regional powers, Deva Raya’s strategic and \nadministrative prowess allowed him to navigate and \nassert the Vijayanagara Kingdom’s influence during \nhis reign.\n• Deva Raya I played a crucial role in modernizing the \nVijayanagara army during his reign. To enhance its \ncapabilities, he focused on improvements in cavalry, \nincorporating Turkic archers, and sourcing horses \nfrom Arabia and Persia. This strategic approach not \nonly strengthened the military but also showcased \nhis commitment to adopting diverse and advanced \nmilitary techniques.\n Infrastructure Builder: Deva Raya I was committed \nto improving irrigation infrastructure in his kingdom. \nHis reign saw notable accomplishments, including the \nconstruction of a dam across the Tungabhadra River, \nwhich facilitated the development of canals to address \nwater shortages in the city. Additionally, he undertook \nthe construction of a dam on the Haridra River, primarily \naimed at enhancing irrigation capabilities. \n Secular Administration: In administrative matters, Deva \nRaya I maintained a secular attitude, exemplified by his \ninclusion of 10,000 Muslims in his army—making him the \nfirst Vijayanagara king to do so. His administration went \nfurther to accommodate the needs of these Muslim \nsoldiers by constructing a mosque and a slaughterhouse \nfor their convenience.\n Foreign Travelers: Notably, during Deva Raya-I’s reign, \nthe Italian traveller Nicolo Conti visited Vijayanagara \naround 1420, and the Russian merchant Nikitin, the \nauthor of ‘Voyage to India’, also documented his \nobservations. \n• Conti remarked on Vijayanagara and Deva Raya I, \nnoting that “In this city, there are 90,000 men fit \nto bear arms. their king is more powerful than all \nthe kings of India.” This reflects the grandeur and \nstrength of Vijayanagara under Deva Raya I, both in \nterms of military prowess and political influence.\n Contributions to Literature: Deva Raya I distinguished \nhimself as a patron of Kannada literature, contributing \nsignificantly to the cultural and artistic legacy of the \nVijayanagara Empire. In his court, there existed a pearl \nhall where he honored individuals of eminence, fostering \nan environment conducive to the flourishing of arts and \nletters.\n• Among the notable figures in his court was Madhura, \na renowned Jain poet who also served in the court \nof Deva Raya I’s father, King Harihara II. Madhura \ncomposed the “Dharmanathapurana,” a work \nin Kannada that narrates the life of the 15th Jain \nTirthankar. Additionally, he penned a poem in praise \nof Gommateshvara of Shravanabelagola, showcasing \nhis literary contributions to the court.\n Contributions to Architecture: Deva Raya I’s patronage \nextended to architectural endeavors, with the \nconstruction of the Hazare Rama temple standing out as \nan excellent example of Deccan architecture. \n• Under his rule, the capital city of the Vijayanagara \nkingdom flourished and expanded, becoming one of \nthe largest cities in the 15th century. \nDeva Raya II (c.1425–46 CE)\nFig 5.4: Empire of Deva Raya-II\n The most illustrious ruler of the Sangama dynasty, Deva \nRaya II, was a multifaceted leader known for his prowess \nin administration, military strategy, and literary pursuits. \nBeyond being an ambitious warrior, he demonstrated a \nkeen interest in literature and philosophy. \n Military Successes: Despite facing occasional setbacks, \nDeva Raya II succeeded in expanding and maintaining \ncontrol over territories that stretched up to the Krishna \nRiver. \n• According to the Persian chronicler Abdur Razzak, \nwho visited during this period, Deva Raya II’s empire \nextended from Ceylon to Gulbarga, encompassing \nOrissa to Malabar."]
