    body: ["Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms 77\n• Krishna Deva Raya restored the Bahmani Sultanate \nunder Muhammad Shah, earning the title of \n‘Yavanarajya sthapanacharya’, meaning ‘the \nestablisher of the Muslim kingdom’.\n• In Orissa’s campaign, he defeated the Gajapathi\nruler Prataparudra and conquered the entirety of \nTelangana. He compelled the ruler of Orissa to return \nto Vijayanagara all territories up to the Krishna River. \n Relation with Portuguese: While maintaining law and \norder, Krishna Deva Raya also addressed the growing \nPortuguese influence in the Deccan. Along with that, he \nmaintained cordial relations with the Portuguese, with \nKing Albuquerque sending ambassadors to his court. \nPortuguese travellers Domingo Paes and Duarto Barbosa \nvisited India during his reign.\n Contributions to Art, Religion, and Architecture: \n• He established Nagalapuram, a suburban township \nnear Vijayanagar, in honor of his mother. \n• As a patron of the arts, Krishna Deva Raya \ncommissioned the construction of several magnificent \ntemples, including the renowned Vittalaswamy and\nHazara Ramaswamy temples in Vijayanagar. \n• He also built the Vijaya Mahal (House of Victory). \n• His dedication to preserving and enhancing religious \nstructures extended to the restoration of numerous \ntemples and the construction of impressive\ngopurams, or gateways, at several prominent \nSouth Indian temples. The renowned temple of \nTirupati flourished during his reign as he held a deep \nreverence for the deity enshrined there. \n Secular Approach: Despite his strong Vaishnava faith, \nKrishna Deva Raya extended respect to all religions, \nfostering a harmonious coexistence within his kingdom. \n Literature and Language: \n• His court was adorned by eight eminent Telugu \nscholars, collectively known as the ‘Ashta diggajas’, \nwho made significant contributions to Telugu \nliterature. Among these luminaries: \n i. Allasani Peddanna- also revered as ‘Andhrakavita \nPitamaha’, works include Manucharitam and \nHarikathasaram.\n ii. Tenali Ramakrishna- another distinguished \nscholar, authored the revered work Panduranga \nMahamatyam. \n iii. Pingali Suranna- a prolific writer, enriched \nTelugu literature with his works Garuda \nPuranam, Prabhavatee Pradyumnamu- Raghava \nPandaveeyam, and Kalapurnodayamu. \n iv. Nandi Timmana- Timmana, famed for \n“Parijataparahana” on Krishna’s celestial heist. \nA master of simple, graceful verse, he excelled \nin the amorous realm, earning the nickname \n“Mukku Thimmana,” “Thimmana of the Nose.\n v. Maadayagari Mallana- He is best known for \nhis Rajasekhara Charitra, a poem about the life \nof King Rajasekhara of Avanti. Mallana’s other \nworks include Krishna Charitra, a biography of \nKrishna, and Narasimha Charitra, a biography \nof Narasimha, an incarnation of Vishnu. He was \nalso a master of the sringara rasa or the erotic \nsentiment.\n vi. Dhoorjati - He questioned societal norms, \nscoffed at materialism, and yearned for \nspiritual liberation. His magnum opus, “Sri \nKalahasteeswara Mahatyam,” hails Lord Shiva, \nshowcasing Dhurjati’s deep devotion. He wasn’t \nshy about critiquing even the king, earning him \nthe title “Kavi Rakshasudu,” the demon poet!\n vii. Ayyala Raaju Ramabhadrudu - He is best known \nfor his “Krishna Gatha”, a poem about the life \nof Krishna. Ramaabhyudayamu was a poem \ncomposed by Ayyalaraju Ramabhadradu in the \n16th century. It tells the story of Rama, from his \nbirth to his ascension to the throne of Ayodhya, in \naround 18 cantos and over 18,000 verses.\n viii. Ramarajabhushanudu - Ramarajabhushanudu’s \nmost famous works are Vasucharitra, Hariscandra \nNalopakhyanamu, and Kavyalankarasangraha.\n◊ Vasucharitra is a poem about the life of Vasu, \na king who is cursed to become a snake. The \npoem is full of vivid descriptions of love, \nadventure, and tragedy.\n◊ Hariscandra Nalopakhyanamu is a poem that \ntells the stories of two kings, Hariscandra and \nNala. The poem is notable for its use of ślesha, \nor double entendre.\n◊ Kavyalankarasangraha is a treatise on poetics. \nIt is considered one of the most important \nworks of Telugu poetry.\n◊ Krishna Deva Raya himself made notable \nliterary contributions, including the Telugu \nmasterpiece Amukthamalyadha and the \nSanskrit works Jambavati Kalyanam and \nUshaparinayam.\n Legacy: Krishna Deva Raya’s reign marked a period of \nunprecedented prosperity, cultural flourishing, and \nmilitary dominance for the Vijayanagara empire. His \nlegacy as an astute ruler, a patron of the arts, and a \ndefender of his kingdom continues to inspire generations.\nAchyuta Deva Raya (c.1529 –1542 CE)\n Achyuta Deva Raya’s Ascension: The younger sibling of \nKrishna Deva Raya, Achyuta Deva Raya, was personally \nchosen as his heir. \n Visit of Portuguese Explorer: During Achyuta Deva \nRaya’s rule, a Portuguese explorer named Fernoa Nuniz \nvisited India."]
