    body: ["78 Medieval India \n Patronage of Arts and Culture: Achyuta Deva Raya \ngenerously supported notable individuals, including \nKannada poet Chatu Vittalanatha, musician Purandaradasa, \nand Sanskrit scholar Rajanatha Dindima II. \n Architectural Endeavours: Architectural achievements \nand cultural developments during Achyuta Deva \nRaya’s leadership include the construction of the \nTiruvengalanatha temple, now known as the \nAchyutaraya temple. \n Succession Dispute and Venkata I’s Rule: After Achyuta \nDeva Raya’s demise, a succession dispute emerged. \nVenkata I assumed the throne but faced challenges as a \nruler, leading to his untimely end just six months later. \n Sada Siva Raya and Regency of Aravidu Aliya Rama \nRaya: Following Venkata I, Krishna Deva Raya’s son Sada \nSiva Raya ascended the throne as a young child under \nthe regency of Aravidu Aliya Rama Raya. \n Rama Raya’s Influence and Achievements: Rama \nRaya’s role is noted as that of a skilled military general, \nefficient administrator, and adept diplomat. He launched \nsuccessful campaigns under Krishna Deva Raya and began \nto exert significant influence over the state’s affairs after \nhis father-in-law’s demise.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the Portuguese influence in the Deccan \nduring Krishna Deva Raya’s reign had a positive or \nnegative impact on the socio-economic and political \ndynamics of the Vijayanagara Empire?\nSadaSiva Raya (c.1542–1570 CE)\n The last ruler of the Tuluva dynasty. \n De Facto Rule of Rama Raya: Sadasiva Raya, the last \nruler of the Tuluva dynasty, served as a mere figurehead \nduring the reign of his powerful minister, Aliya Rama \nRaya, who effectively wielded control as the de facto \nking. Rama Raya implemented a strategy of purging the \nold nobility and replacing them with individuals loyal to \nhim, solidifying his grip on power.\n Over-reliance on Alliance Making: To maintain a balance \nof power among the Deccan sultanates, Rama Raya \nengaged in a series of alliances, strategically pitting one \nsultanate against another to serve his interests. \n• For instance, when the Nizam of Ahmadnagar and\nthe Qutab Shahi of Golconda sought Rama Raya’s \nassistance against Bijapur, he secured the Raichur \nDoab for his allies. \n• Around 1549 CE, when the Adil Shahi of Bijapur \nand the Barid Shahi of Bidar declared war on the \nNizam Shahi of Ahmadnagar, Rama Raya intervened \non behalf of Ahmadnagar and captured the fort of \nKalyana.\n Alliances Proved Futile: Rama Raya’s relentless pursuit \nof self-serving political alliances, constantly switching \nsides to advance his position, ultimately prompted the \nDeccan sultanates to unite against him. \n• Battle of Talikota: In 1565 CE, the Deccan states of \nBijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golkonda, and Bidar, excluding \nBerar, formed a formidable confederacy and inflicted \na devastating defeat upon the Vijayanagara armies \nat the Battle of Talikota, also known as Rakshasa \nThangadi, or Battle of Bannihatti. This momentous \nbattle marked the downfall of the once-glorious \nVijayanagara empire. \n• Rama Raya was captured and executed, and the city \nof Vijayanagar, the empire’s capital, was plundered \nand destroyed. \n• It is worth noting that Caesar Frederick, an Italian\ntraveller, visited Vijayanagar after the Battle of \nTalaikotta during 1567-68, witnessing the aftermath \nof the empire’s collapse.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nWhat points can you put forth to support the \nargument that the Battle of Talikota in 1565 CE was \nnot just a military defeat for the Vijayanagar Empire \nbut also a cultural and intellectual setback for South \nIndia.\nARAVIDU DYNASTY (C.1570-1650)\nThey ruled from Penukonda and later from Chanragiri (near \nTirupati) for another century.\n Thirumala, Sri Ranga and Venkata II held significance as \nrulers during this dynasty’s rule. \n The last ruler of Vijayanagar kingdom was Sri Ranga III \n(c.1642–1646 CE).\nIMPORTANT ASPECTS OF \nVIJAYANAGAR EMPIRE\nAdministration of Vijaynagar Kingdom\n Judicial System and King’s Authority: The Vijayanagar \nkingdom boasted a well-organized administration where \nthe Rayas wielded absolute authority in executive, \njudicial, and legislative matters, functioning as the \nhighest court of appeal. The dispensation of justice \ninvolved severe punishments, including mutilation and \nexecution by elephants.\n Succession and Council of Ministers: The succession to \nthe throne was primarily hereditary, although instances \nof usurpation occurred. The king received support from \na council of ministers in daily governance, ensuring a \nbalanced approach to decision-making."]
