    id: "m5-pdf-page-88",
    chapter: "05 • Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 88 — Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms 87  It ruled the Sultanate of Bijapur in present-day Bijapur district, Karnataka, in the Western Deccan region of",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms 87\n It ruled the Sultanate of Bijapur in present-day Bijapur \ndistrict, Karnataka, in the Western Deccan region of \nSouthern India from 1489 to 1686.\n Bijapur was previously a province of the Bahmani \nSultanate before its political decline and eventual \nabsorption into the Mughal Empire in 1686.\nYusuf Adil Shah\n Yusuf Adil Shah (1490–1510), the dynasty’s founder, \nwas initially appointed governor of the province by \nthe Bahmani rulers before establishing a de facto \nindependent Bijapur state.\n He built the world’s second￾largest dome (Gol Gumbaz of \nBijapur).\n Both Yusuf and his son Ismail \ncommonly adopted the title \nAdil Khan.\n In various Central Asian \ncultures, ‘Khan’ denoted \n‘Chief’ and was assimilated \ninto Persian culture.\n It implied a lower status compared to ‘Shah’, which \nsignified royal rank.\n It wasn’t until the reign of Yusuf’s grandson, Ibrahim Adil \nShah I (1534–1558), that the title of Adil Shah became \nprevalent. Despite this, The Bijapur rulers acknowledged \nSafavid Persian suzerainty over their domain.\nIbrahim Adil Shah II\n Ibrahim Adil Shah II, the fifth king of the Adil Shahi \ndynasty (c.1580–1627 CE), was a notable ruler famous \nfor his religious tolerance. \n He earned the title “Jagadguru Budshah” due to his \ncommitment to secularism. Ibrahim Adil Shah II aimed \nto foster cultural harmony between Shias and Sunnis, \nas well as Hindus and Muslims, particularly through the \nmedium of music. \n A passionate lover of music, he played musical \ninstruments, sang, and composed praises for Hindu \ndeities such as Saraswati and Ganapati.\n In his efforts to promote cultural integration, Ibrahim \nAdil Shah II maintained a large number of musicians, \napproximately 4000, at his court. \n This group of musicians was known as “Lashkar-e￾Nauras” (Army of Nauras), and they received regular \npayments from the government. Ibrahim Adil Shah II \neven authored the book “Kitab-E-Navras” (Book of Nine \nRasas) in Dakhani, reflecting his commitment to cultural \ndiversity and inclusivity.\nTerritorial Expansion\n The territorial boundaries of the Bijapur Sultanate \nunderwent significant changes over time.\n While its Northern border remained relatively \nstable, encompassing parts of present-day Southern \nMaharashtra and Northern Karnataka, the Sultanate \nexpanded southward.\nFig 5.9: Gol Gumbaz, \nBijapur\n The conquest of the Raichur Doab followed the \nVijayanagara Empire’s defeat at the Battle of Talikota in \n1565.\n Subsequent campaigns, particularly during the reign of \nMohammed Adil Shah (1627–1657), further extended \nBijapur’s formal borders and nominal authority, reaching \nas far south as Bangalore.\n To the west, Bijapur was bounded by the Portuguese \nstate of Goa, while the Sultanate of Golconda, ruled by \nthe Qutb Shahi dynasty, marked its Eastern boundary.\nCapital and Architectural Development\n Bijapur, the former Bahmani provincial capital, remained \nthe Sultanate’s capital.\n Ibrahim Adil Shah I and Ali Adil Shah I initiated significant \narchitectural enhancements, including fortifications, \nmosques, palaces, and water supply infrastructure.\n Successive rulers continued to embellish Bijapur with \nmagnificent structures, showcasing Deccan Sultanate \nand Indo-Islamic architecture.\nDecline of the Sultanate\n Bijapur was embroiled in the instability and turmoil that \nensued following the collapse of the Bahmani Empire.\n Persistent conflicts, including battles with the \nVijayanagara Empire and other Deccan Sultanates, \nhindered the state’s progress until the Deccan Sultanates \nunited to defeat Vijayanagara at Talikota in 1565. \nSubsequently, Bijapur seized control of the neighbouring \nSultanate of Bidar in 1619.\n The Portuguese Empire applied pressure on Goa, a major \nport under the Adil Shahi rule, until its conquest during \nIbrahim II’s reign. \n Despite facing challenges, Bijapur experienced relative \nstability until Shivaji’s rebellion.\n Shivaji, whose father served as a Maratha commander \nunder Adil Shah, established an independent Maratha \nKingdom that eventually evolved into the Maratha \nEmpire, one of India’s largest before British colonisation.\n The primary threat to Bijapur’s security arose in the late \n16th century with the Mughal Empire’s expansion into \nthe Deccan. \n While the Mughals may have ultimately contributed to \nthe downfall of the Adil Shahi dynasty, it was Shivaji’s \nuprising that significantly weakened their control.\n Through various agreements and treaties, Mughal \nsuzerainty over the Adil Shahs gradually increased, \nleading to Bijapur’s formal recognition of Mughal \nauthority in 1636. \n The demands imposed by their Mughal overlords depleted \nthe Adil Shahs’ resources, ultimately culminating in the \nMughal conquest of Bijapur in 1686 during the reign of \nAurangzeb.\nArt and Architecture\n The Adil Shahi Sultans focused extensively on architecture \nand related arts, with each Sultan striving to surpass"]
