    id: "m5-pdf-page-89",
    chapter: "05 • Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 89 — 88 Medieval India his predecessor in the scale, grandeur, and number of architectural endeavours.",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["88 Medieval India \nhis predecessor in the scale, grandeur, and number of \narchitectural endeavours.\n Bijapur’s architectural style is a blend of Persian, \nOttoman Turkish, and Deccani influences, showcased in \nstructures like Ibrahim Rouzah, Dilkusha Mahal, Malika￾e-Jahan Mosque, and Jal Mahal. Skilled Bijapur sculptors \nadorned these monuments with intricate stone carvings \nakin to woodwork, while stucco plaster designs exhibited \nexceptional craftsmanship.\n The Adil Shahi kings made significant contributions to \nKarnataka’s architecture, painting, language, literature, \nand music, transforming Bijapur into a cosmopolitan hub \nthat attracted scholars, artists, musicians, and Sufi saints \nfrom diverse regions such as Turkey, Persia (Iran), Iraq, \nand Turkestan.\n The unfinished Jami Masjid, initiated in 1565, features an \narcaded prayer hall supported by substantial piers and \ncrowned with an impressive dome. \n The Ibrahim Rouza, housing the tomb of Ibrahim Adil \nShah II, showcases intricate carvings executed by Persian \nartists from the Adil Shahi court, some of which are \npreserved in prominent European museums.\n The Dakhani language, a fusion of Persian-Arabic, Urdu, \nMarathi, and Kannada, emerged as a distinct spoken and \nliterary language under the Adil Shahis. \n Literary works, including Ibrahim Adil Shah II’s Kitab-e￾Navras containing poems and music, were published in \nDakhani. \n The Mushaira, a poetic symposium, originated in the \nBijapur court and later spread northward. Over time, \nDakhani evolved into Dakhan Urdu to differentiate it \nfrom North Indian Urdu.\n3. Imad Shahis of Berar\n The Berar Sultanate, known as the Imad Shahi Sultanate, \nemerged as one of the Deccan sultanates, initiated by an \nIndian Muslim leader.\n Its establishment in 1490 followed the fragmentation of \nthe Bahmani Sultanate.\n It was founded by Fatullah Khan Imad-ul-Mulk in 1490.\n Fath-Ullah Imad-ul-Mulk established the capital of the \nBerar Sultanate at Achalpur and also fortified Gavilgad \nand Narnala.\nFig 5.10: Gavilgad Fort, built by \nFathullah Imad-ul-Mulk of Berar\n Ala-ud-din, Imad-ul-Mulk’s eldest son, succeeded him \nafter his death in 1504.\n Ala-ud-din repulsed Ahmadnagar’s invasion in 1528 with \nthe aid of Gujarati King Bahadur Shah. \n In an effort to stop Ahmadnagar’s aggression, the \nfollowing queen of Berar, Darya, initially tried to form an \nalliance with Bijapur, but she was unsuccessful.\n However, it was absorbed by Ahmednagar during the \ninvasion of 1574.\n4. Qutub Shahis of Golconda\n The Qutb Shahi dynasty, originating from Turkoman \ndescent, was a Persianate Shia Islamic ruling family that \ngoverned the Sultanate of Golkonda in southern India.\n Established in 1512 AD by Sultan-Quli Qutb-ul-Mulk, \ncommonly known, albeit inaccurately, as “Quli Qutb \nShah”, following the dissolution of the Bahmani Sultanate.\n Golconda, along with the establishment of Char Minar \nwhich later became Hyderabad, functioned as the \ncapitals of the sultanate, and both cities were enhanced \nby the efforts of the Qutb Shahi sultans. \n The dynasty governed Golconda for 171 years until the \nDeccan was subdued by the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb \nin 1687.\nSultan-Quli Qutb-ul-Mulk\n The dynasty’s founder, Sultan Quli Khawas Khan Hamdani, \nhailed from Hamadan Province, Iran, and belonged to \nthe Qara Qoyunlu, an Iranian Turkmen Muslim tribe. He \nwas a descendant of Qara Yusuf.\n In the 16th century, Sultan Quli migrated to Delhi \nalongside his uncle, Allah-Quli, along with several \nrelatives and companions. Later, he ventured south to \nthe Deccan region and served under the Bahmani sultan, \nMahmood Shah Bahmani II, who belonged to the Deccani \nMuslim ethnicity.\n Sultan Quli declared the independence of Golconda \nsubsequent to the fragmentation of the Bahmani \nKingdom into the five Deccan sultanates.\n Following this declaration, he asserted independence \nfrom the Bahmani Sultanate, assuming the title Qutub \nShah, and founding the Qutb Shahi dynasty of Golconda.\n Tragically, Sultan Quli was assassinated in 1543 by his \nson, Jamsheed, who then ascended to the sultanate.\n Jamsheed succumbed to cancer in 1550, after which \nhis young son briefly ruled for a year. Subsequently, the \nnobility reinstated Ibrahim Quli as sultan.\nArt and Architecture\n The Qutub Shahis of Golconda played a significant role in \nthe history of the Deccan region. \n The Qutb Shahis were patrons of Persianate Shia culture, \nemphasising Persian culture during the initial 90 years of \ntheir rule (circa 1512–1600)."]
