    id: "m5-pdf-page-90",
    chapter: "05 • Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms",
    title: "PDF पृष्ठ 90 — Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms 89  Persian was the official language of their court, and they fostered Persian culture and literature, establishing a",
    subtitle: "मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",
    body: ["Vijayanagar Empire and Bahamani Kingdoms 89\n Persian was the official language of their court, and they \nfostered Persian culture and literature, establishing a \nsanctuary for Persian culture under Quli Qutb Mulk.\n In the early 17th century, during the reign of Sultan \nMuhammad Quli Qutb Shah (1580–1612), a shift \noccurred as he began to support Telugu language and \nculture alongside Persian.\n Edicts started to be issued in both Persian and Telugu, \nreflecting the evolving cultural landscape.\n Towards the dynasty’s end, official decrees were primarily \nin Telugu, with Persian summaries, indicating a transition \nto embracing Telugu identity.\n Sultan Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah (1580–1612) \ncomposed poetry in Dakhini Urdu, Persian, and Telugu, \nshowcasing the linguistic diversity of the era.\n Later poets and writers predominantly wrote in Urdu \nwhile incorporating vocabulary from Persian, Hindi, and \nTelugu languages.\n In 1634 AD, during Abdullah Qutb Shah’s reign, the \nancient Sanskrit text on love and sex, Ratirahasya by \nKokkoka, was translated into Persian and renamed \nLazzat-un-Nisa (Flavors of the Woman).\n Quli Qutub Shah is renowned for constructing the famous \nGolconda Fort. However, it’s important to note that the \ngreatest of all Qutub Shahi rulers was Mohammed Quli \nQutub Shah, who left a lasting legacy in the region.\n Mohammed Quli Qutub Shah is best known for \nbuilding the city of Hyderabad, a major urban center \nthat continues to be a prominent city in modern India. \nAdditionally, he is credited with the construction of the \niconic monument, the “Charminar,” which stands as a \nsymbol of Hyderabad’s cultural and historical richness. \nMohammed Quli Qutub Shah’s contributions have \nleft an indelible mark on the architectural and cultural \nlandscape of the Deccan.\n5. Barid Shahis of Bidar\n Bidar, the smallest among the five Deccan sultanates, \nwas established by Qasim Barid I, a Georgian who was \nenslaved by Turks.\n Initially serving in the Bahmani ruler Mahmud Shah \nBahmani’s administration as a sar-naubat (commander), \nhe later rose to the position of mir-jumla (governor) \nwithin the Bahmani Sultanate.\n In 1492, he assumed the de facto leadership of Bahmani, \nalthough Sultan Mahmud Shah Bahmani retained the \nnominal title of ruler.\n After Mahmud Shah Bahmani’s demise in 1504, his \nson Amir Barid took charge of the Bahmani Sultanate’s \nadministration.\n In 1528, following the departure of the last Bahmani \nruler, Kalimullah, from Bidar, Amir Barid effectively \ngoverned as an independent ruler.\n Amir Barid was succeeded by his son Ali Barid, who was the \nfirst to adopt the title of Shah. He participated in the Battle \nof Talikota and had a passion for poetry and calligraphy.\n The final ruler of the Bidar Sultanate, Amir Barid Shah III, \nsuffered defeat in 1619, leading to the annexation of the \nsultanate by the Bijapur Sultanate.\nStep Wells within the Golconda Fort\n The Qutb Shahi \ntombs’ step wells \nwithin the Golconda \nfort in Telangana \nreceived UNESCO’s \nAsia-Pacific award \nin 2022 for the \npreservation of \ncultural heritage.\n Dating back to the 16th century, these step wells, \ncrafted in Persian style, have been honored with \nthe Award of Distinction among the six categories \nof recognition, which include Award of Excellence, \nAward of Merit, Award for New Design in Heritage \nContexts, and Special Recognition for Sustainable \nDevelopment.\nFig 5.11: Stepwells of \nGolconda\nFort\nAmong these, the realms of Ahmadnagar, Bijapur, and \nGolconda held prominent positions in Deccan politics until \ntheir assimilation into the Mughal Empire in the 17th century.\nCONCLUSION\nThe Vijayanagar Empire and the Bahmani Kingdoms, \npivotal players in the Deccan’s history, left an indelible \nmark. Vijayanagar, centered in Hampi, adorned the region \nwith cultural and architectural brilliance, evident in ornate \ntemples and vibrant traditions. Concurrently, the Bahmani \nKingdoms, a blend of sultanates, showcased artistic \nflourishing in Bijapur and Golconda, reflecting diverse \ninfluences. The clash between Vijayanagar and Bahmani at \nthe Battle of Talikota in 1565 marked a turning point, leading \nto Vijayanagar’s decline. Both entities grappled with internal \nstrife and external pressures, illustrating the intricate \ngeopolitical landscape of medieval South India. Their \nenduring legacy is seen in the cultural amalgamation shaping \nthe Deccan’s art, architecture, and traditions, offering insight \ninto the nuanced history of medieval South Indian politics \nand cultural evolution."]
